{"causes":["The rise of Romanticism in the early 19th century spurred interest in folklore and tradition.","The Brothers Grimm began their collection of German fairy tales in the early 1800s, aiming to preserve cultural heritage.","Pre-Christian European beliefs in nature spirits and deities influenced the character of Mother Hulda."],"effects":["Mother Hulda's tale has been adapted into numerous cultural forms, including literature, theater, and film.","The moral lessons embedded in the story have influenced educational narratives in folklore and child-rearing practices.","Mother Hulda's legacy has contributed to the discussion of feminine archetypes in folklore and literature."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"brothers-grimm","sourceName":"Brothers Grimm","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"mother-hulda","targetName":"Mother Hulda","context":"In 1812, the Brothers Grimm included Mother Hulda in their collection of fairy tales, shaping her narrative for future generations."},{"sourceSlug":"german-folklore","sourceName":"German Folklore","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"mother-hulda","targetName":"Mother Hulda","context":"Mother Hulda is deeply rooted in German folklore, influencing cultural perceptions of femininity and domesticity."},{"sourceSlug":"germany","sourceName":"Germany","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"mother-hulda","targetName":"Mother Hulda","context":"The tale of Mother Hulda originates from Germany, reflecting the cultural and social values of the region during the early 19th century."},{"sourceSlug":"winter-solstice","sourceName":"Winter Solstice","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"mother-hulda","targetName":"Mother Hulda","context":"The themes of Mother Hulda's narrative align with winter solstice traditions, emphasizing the cycle of seasons and nature."},{"sourceSlug":"feminine-archetypes","sourceName":"Feminine Archetypes","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"mother-hulda","targetName":"Mother Hulda","context":"Mother Hulda serves as a defining figure in discussions of feminine archetypes within folklore, representing both nurturing and punishing aspects."}],"places":[{"name":"Hanau, Germany","role":"Collection site"},{"name":"Grimm Brothers' Home, Kassel, Germany","role":"Major influence"},{"name":"Bavaria, Germany","role":"Cultural origin"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Mother Hulda","type":"Reference article","slug":"mother-hulda"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11853","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frau_Holle"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Mother Hulda contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Mother Hulda (German: Frau Holle) is a German fairy tale recorded by the Brothers Grimm in Kinder- und Hausmärchen ('Children's and Household Tales') as tale KHM 24, in the first edition (1812), and one of the most significant tales in the Grimm collection from a folkloric and religious history perspective. The tale narrates the story of a widow's two daughters — a diligent, good-natured stepdaughter and an idle, vain biological daughter — who fall into a well and find themselves in an underground realm ruled by Mother Hulda (Frau Holle); the good stepdaughter works hard for Mother Hulda and is rewarded with a shower of gold when she returns to the upper world; the idle daughter shirks her work and is covered in pitch (black tar) as her reward.

Mother Hulda is far more significant than its modest fairy tale frame suggests — Frau Holle (or Holda, Hulda, Perchta) is a major Germanic folk deity, associated with winter (when she shakes out her feather bed, snow falls on earth), spinning (she is the patron of spinning women), the dead (she leads a 'wild hunt' of the souls of the dead), and the underworld. She is one of the few Germanic pre-Christian deities to survive in continuous folk tradition to the modern period, preserved in fairy tale disguise in the Grimm collection and in folk customs across Germany, Austria, and Switzerland.

The Grimm brothers were aware of Frau Holle's mythological significance — Jacob Grimm's Deutsche Mythologie (German Mythology, 1835) discusses her in detail, identifying her with the Germanic goddess Holda and connecting her to Norse mythology (possibly to Frigg or Hel). Mother Hulda/Frau Holle is thus one of the most important survivals of Germanic pre-Christian religious tradition in the fairy tale form, and the tale is a unique document of the Christianisation of Germanic religious culture — the goddess transformed into a fairy tale helper, her religious functions (spinner, ruler of the dead, winter goddess) preserved in attenuated form.","subjects":["German Literature","19th Century","Fairy Tale","Brothers Grimm","Germanic Religion","Folk Literature","Pre-Christian Religion","Mythology"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Mother Hulda's tale reflects significant cultural values of early 19th-century Germany, influencing gender roles and moral education through folklore, which has persisted across generations and cultures.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
